WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.410
Hi, I'm Shi Feng.

2
00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:05.650
In this video, we will talk about minimum perfect matching problem.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:11.590
In particular, I want to share the main result and the ideas from this paper.

4
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.710
It studies the variance of the minimum perfect matching cost.

5
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.670
I will explain the result and the intuition behind its proof.

6
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:29.780
The later intuition assumes some familiarity with standard probability tools, including the Efron–Stein inequality and the law of total variance.

7
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:36.470
Familiarity with the Poincaré inequality is helpful but not required to understand the intuition.

8
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.370
Here is the setup.

9
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:46.670
Consider an even number n of points, sampled independently and uniformly in a unit square with side length one.

10
00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:55.010
Our goal is to pair the points so that every point has exactly one partner and the total pairing distance is minimized.

11
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.110
This means n/2 disjoint pairs with nobody left out or used twice.

12
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:10.310
The left picture is a valid pairing, though not optimal. 100 points form 50 pairs. The right picture is not valid.

13
00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:14.450
The red points have no partner because their connection is missing.

14
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.310
The orange points each have two partners because an extra connection was added.

15
00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.630
Either problem breaks the rule.

16
00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.050
Let's define the problem clearly.

17
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.470
Let X_1 to X_n be the random points.

18
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.870
We write H_n for the minimum total pairing cost.

19
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:36.710
In the formula, m represents a valid pairing of the indices 1 to n.

20
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.710
It groups them into disjoint unordered pairs.

21
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:48.810
Here, d(X_i, X_j) is the distance between the two points using ordinary Euclidean distance in our square.

22
00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.430
The sum counts each pair once.

23
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.410
The minimum chooses the best way of pairing.

24
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.870
Here, n is the total number of points.

25
00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.330
The picture shows the optimal pairing for these locations.

26
00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:07.910
For the 100 points shown here, the minimum total pairing cost is about 3.364.

27
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.890
The same questions can also be asked in higher dimensions.

28
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.210
The square becomes the cube in three dimensions.

29
00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:20.930
More generally, we use the d-dimensional unit cube, written [0,1]^d.

30
00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:24.190
Every coordinate lies between 0 and 1.

31
00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:29.440
We again sample an even number n of points, independently and uniformly.

32
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.790
The goal is unchanged.

33
00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:37.230
Divide the points into pairs and minimize the sum of Euclidean distance.

34
00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.090
We still call the minimum H_n.

35
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:43.870
We will consider dimensions d larger or equal to 2.

36
00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.170
Higher dimensions are hard to visualize.

37
00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:54.070
So from here on, we will use two-dimensional pictures while keeping d in the formulas for the general dimensions.

38
00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.850
Because the point locations are random, the minimum matching cost H_n is also random.

39
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:06.030
Now, let the number of points become large. We ask three questions.

40
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:11.690
How does the expectation or average cost depend on the number of points?

41
00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.340
How large is the spread around the average measured by the variance?

42
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:23.130
And after subtracting the average and rescaling the fluctuations, is there a limiting distribution?

43
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.190
Let's first look at the expectation.

44
00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.550
Choose one point and ask: how far away is the nearest other point?

45
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:38.690
In a fixed dimension d, a ball of radius r has volume of order r to the power of d.

46
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:48.230
Among n uniform points, we expect about n r to the power of d others in that neighborhood, up to dimension-dependent constant.

47
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:56.310
The first neighbor appears when this count is of order 1, suggesting distance n to the power of negative 1 over d.

48
00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.570
There are n over 2 pairs.

49
00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:06.940
Multiplying by this nearest-neighbor scale suggests total cost of order n to the power of 1 minus 1 over d.

50
00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.890
This is a scale heuristic, not a matching algorithm.

51
00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.740
Choosing everyone's nearest neighbor need not give a valid pairing.

52
00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.530
Classical results confirm the exponent.

53
00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:27.210
The expectation is asymptotic to beta d times n to the power of 1 minus 1 over d, for some positive constant beta d.

54
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.010
Next, the variance.

55
00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.850
In dimension 2, Rhee's upper bound grows like log n squared.

56
00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:41.030
In higher dimension, Talagrand obtained n to the power of 1 minus 2 over d with an extra log factor.

57
00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.090
Our paper removes those losses.

58
00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:53.500
The variance is at most C d n to the power of 1 minus 2 over d, with C independent of n and d.

59
00:04:53.500 --> 00:05:01.550
In the square, substituting d equal to 2 into the formula gives a variance bounded by a constant C.

60
00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:09.066
The predicted lower bound is c_d n to the power of 1 minus 2 over d with some c_d larger than 0,

61
00:05:09.066 --> 00:05:12.410
matching the upper bound order for each fixed dimension.

62
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.500
There is an interesting story behind the lower bound.

63
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:23.750
In Talagrand's paper, he suggested the lower bound as an exercise, explicitly noting that it was unchecked.

64
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:31.470
Later, Chatterjee listed a tight lower bound for matching fluctuations with uniform points as an open problem.

65
00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.350
In this project, we focus only on the upper bound.

66
00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.210
What about the shape of limiting distribution?

67
00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:41.900
The limiting distribution remains open for this model.

68
00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:46.630
My guess is a standard Gaussian limit shown here as a conjecture.

69
00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:50.210
The simulation on the right also supports this guess.

70
00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:54.390
The standardized matching costs look close to a Gaussian distribution.

71
00:05:55.220 --> 00:06:03.310
I won't explain the reason behind this guess here, because it would involve several abstract ideas and predictions that have not been validated.

72
00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.690
If you are interested in exploring this further,

73
00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:14.250
I encourage you to read my paper on quadratic matching on the flat torus in dimension d larger than 5.

74
00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:21.210
It explains the property and estimates needed to prove the central limit theorem in a related model.

75
00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:25.140
For the rest of the video, we will focus on the variance.

76
00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:29.830
For simplicity, fix dimension d and study dependence on n.

77
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:37.230
Our goal is an upper bound C_d times n to the power of 1 minus 2 over d, where C_d may depend on d.

78
00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.030
First, try the Efron–Stein inequality.

79
00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:48.110
Replace xi by an independent uniform point xi prime, as shown in the picture, leaving the other points fixed.

80
00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.910
Call the new minimum H_n^i.

81
00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:59.702
The Efron–Stein inequality tells us that the variance is bounded by one half of the sum of the expected squared change in the minimum cost,

82
00:06:59.702 --> 00:07:02.530
over all n possible point replacements.

83
00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:05.970
Keep the old partner and every other pair.

84
00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:13.100
The triangle inequality bounds the new edge by the old edge plus the displacement from xi to xi prime.

85
00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.650
The new optimum is no more expensive than this pairing cost.

86
00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.510
Starting from the new optimum gives the reversed bound.

87
00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:27.850
Therefore, the absolute cost change is at most d(X_i, X_i′).

88
00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:35.650
This gives us a bound of C_d n, which is larger than the target bound C_d n to the power of 1 minus 2 over d.

89
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.430
Now, let me explain our method.

90
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:46.830
We begin by partitioning the unit cube into equal cubical cells, choosing the number of cells to be of order n.

91
00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:52.250
The total volume is 1, so each cell has volume of order 1 over n.

92
00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:58.710
Taking the d-th root, its side length ℓ is of order n to the power of negative 1 over d.

93
00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:04.060
Our goal is to show that the variance of H_n is at most C_d nℓ².

94
00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:10.830
For visualization, we use a 3 by 3 partitioning of the square with the same 100 points.

95
00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:16.350
Label the cells from left to right, row by row starting from the upper left side.

96
00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.230
Now we count the number of points in each cell.

97
00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:30.150
For example, the first cell c1 has 16 points, so k1 equals to 16. Every row gives one count.

98
00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.690
Put the nine counts back to the cell.

99
00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:39.210
Together, they form k, read from left to right, row by row, starting from the upper left.

100
00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:45.890
Each kj counts the number of points in the cell cj. Write q for the number of cells.

101
00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.270
Here, q equals to 9.

102
00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.564
Every entry is a nonnegative integer, and the entries sum to n.

103
00:08:53.564 --> 00:09:02.490
Because the points are sampled independently, and each cell have probability 1 over q, K has the multinomial law shown below.

104
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:10.130
Now, condition on a particular count vector K equals k. How can we generate the points?

105
00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:14.730
Start with empty cells, and keep the prescribed count fixed.

106
00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:20.460
In the first cell, sample k1 positions independently and uniformly.

107
00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:30.150
Then do the same in second cells, with k2 points, and continue through the cells row by row. Watch the cells fill one by one.

108
00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:36.070
Keep the points already sampled, and sample the next cell independently of all others.

109
00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:40.010
In our example, the nine counts add up to 100.

110
00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:43.630
So the completed picture has 100 points.

111
00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:48.410
This generates the conditional points configuration with the given counts.

112
00:09:48.980 --> 00:09:57.110
Now apply the Efron–Stein inequality to the independent within cell samples, with the count vector k fixed.

113
00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.610
Resample xi inside its cell to obtain xi prime.

114
00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:05.110
Watch the partner move inside and outside the cell.

115
00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:09.890
Wherever it lies, the triangle inequality gives the same comparison.

116
00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.423
Keep that partner and every other pair.

117
00:10:13.423 --> 00:10:20.450
The new pairing cost is at most the old pairing cost plus the distance between xi and xi prime.

118
00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:25.110
The new optimum is no more expensive than this comparison pairing.

119
00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:28.870
Starting from the new optimum gives the reverse bound.

120
00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:37.730
For optimal total pairing cost, the absolute difference between the old and the new pairing is at most the displacement,

121
00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:41.800
bounded by the cell diameter square root of d times ℓ.

122
00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:48.050
Efron–Stein sums the conditional expected squared distances over all n resamplings.

123
00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:55.410
Each squared change is at most dℓ², so the variance is bounded by the target scale.

124
00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:02.430
The conditional variance bound suggests using the law of total variance. It separates two effects.

125
00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:08.680
The variance of the conditional mean comes first, and the expected conditional variance second.

126
00:11:08.680 --> 00:11:16.830
We bounded the second term by C_d nℓ² in the previous argument, so it remains to bound the first.

127
00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:22.030
The first term can be viewed as a variance of a function on the count state space.

128
00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:28.270
The count states are nonnegative integer vectors with Q entries, summing to n.

129
00:11:29.120 --> 00:11:34.030
Their law π is multinomial with cell probabilities 1 over Q.

130
00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:40.090
Define f(k) to be the expected pairing cost given the count vector to be K.

131
00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:44.610
The first term is variance of F under the multinomial law.

132
00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:50.970
We seek a variance bound of C_d nℓ² for this function on the multinomial count space.

133
00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.610
A standard way to bound the variance of a function is through the Poincaré inequality.

134
00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:02.980
In this approach, the inequality is associated with the choice of dynamics.

135
00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:09.650
For example, Langevin dynamics in Euclidean space or Glauber dynamics on the Boolean cube.

136
00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:16.410
Here, a random walk on the count state space can be visualized by moving points between the cells.

137
00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:22.210
Our strategy is the chain of two inequalities on the slide.

138
00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:25.330
The middle quantity is called the Dirichlet energy.

139
00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:34.230
The takeaway is that the Poincaré inequality turns our variance-bound problem into finding a suitable random walk on the count states.

140
00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:41.610
Its stationary distribution should be the multinomial law, and it should satisfy the two inequalities shown here.

141
00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:46.830
What do the two inequalities require of our random walk?

142
00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:55.280
For the first, we want to allow as many transitions as possible, so the walk can spread efficiently through the count space.

143
00:12:55.280 --> 00:13:00.930
For the second, we want each allowed transition to change f only a little.

144
00:13:01.360 --> 00:13:06.510
Here, f(k) is the average pairing cost with the counts fixed to be k.

145
00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:09.250
The picture shows one candidate move.

146
00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:12.570
Transfer a point from the first cell to the second.

147
00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:19.770
This subtracts one from the first coordinate of k and adds one in the second coordinate of k.

148
00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:26.270
If f(k) and f(k′) are close, we allow this transition for this random walk.

149
00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:37.070
Our goal is to find as many transitions from k to k prime as possible, for which the absolute difference between f(k) and f(k′) is at most C_d ℓ.

150
00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.810
Let's explore which transitions work.

151
00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:45.920
The first transition is to move one point from cell ci to a neighboring cell cj.

152
00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:56.290
This subtracts one from the ith coordinate of k and adds one to its jth coordinate, giving k prime equals to k minus ei plus ej.

153
00:13:56.660 --> 00:14:04.630
With the count fixed, choose a point x in ci and redraw it in cj, written as x prime.

154
00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:08.890
The original partner z of x can lie anywhere.

155
00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:13.880
Start from an optimal pairing and keep that partner and all other pairs.

156
00:14:13.880 --> 00:14:18.810
As the point moves from x to x prime, only this edge changes.

157
00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:25.750
The triangle inequality bounds the new edge by the old edge plus the displacement between x and x prime.

158
00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:30.370
The new optimum costs no more than this comparison pairing.

159
00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:33.710
Start from the new optimum gives the reverse bound.

160
00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:37.980
So the absolute change in optimal cost is at most the displacement.

161
00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:44.050
This displacement is at most C_d ℓ, regardless where the partner lies.

162
00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:55.870
Finally, averaging over all coupled configuration gives the difference between f(k′) and f(k) less than C_d ℓ, which is small at target scale.

163
00:14:56.660 --> 00:15:03.330
We can therefore allow the random walk to transfer one count between any two neighboring cells that share a side.

164
00:15:03.760 --> 00:15:06.090
Watch the point move one at a time.

165
00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:14.510
Whenever a point arrives in a neighboring cell, the source count decreased by one and the destination count increased by one.

166
00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:16.930
All the other counts stay unchanged.

167
00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.110
The walk then continues from this new configuration.

168
00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.930
The total number of points remain 100 throughout.

169
00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:30.570
The preceding triangle argument bounds the change in expected pairing cost by C_d ℓ.

170
00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.940
This neighboring move gives us a useful local transition for the walk.

171
00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:39.790
The second type of transition is less obvious. It is the pair move.

172
00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:43.870
Consider two cells, no matter how far apart they are.

173
00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:51.410
Choose two points in the source cell ci and redraw them independently and uniformly in another cell cj.

174
00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:56.170
The source count decreased by two and the destination count increased by two.

175
00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:01.330
First, suppose these points are paired together in the original optimum.

176
00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:07.290
Move them together and keep them paired, leaving every other pair unchanged.

177
00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.310
The long travel is not a matching edge.

178
00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.230
We simply replaced one short edge by another.

179
00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:20.410
The absolute change in this comparison cost is the absolute difference between the old and the new edge distance,

180
00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:24.270
bounded by their sum and hence by C_d ℓ.

181
00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:27.540
The new optimum costs no more than this comparison.

182
00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:31.210
So this controls its possible increase in this case.

183
00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:36.190
Now suppose the two selected points have different partners, A and B.

184
00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:40.790
Redraw the points independently and uniformly in the distant cell.

185
00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:48.050
For the new pairing, you connect the old partners and pair the two new points. All other pairs stay unchanged.

186
00:16:48.860 --> 00:16:51.130
Watch the path through the two old points.

187
00:16:51.600 --> 00:17:01.970
By the triangle inequality on the left image, the new edge costs at most the two old edge plus the distance between the two points x1 and x2.

188
00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:11.310
The possible increase in the optimal cost is therefore at most the distance between x1 and x2 plus the distance between y1 and y2.

189
00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:14.710
Which is at most C_d ℓ.

190
00:17:15.140 --> 00:17:17.410
Both pairing cases are now covered.

191
00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:23.420
Start from the new optimum and apply whichever case fits to bound the opposite change.

192
00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:28.290
This gives the same bound on the absolute difference of the optimal cost.

193
00:17:28.900 --> 00:17:37.070
Finally, averaging over coupled configurations gives the target bound on the difference between f(k′) and f(k).

194
00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:39.970
The distance between the cells never enters.

195
00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:53.240
Therefore, we can allow the random walk to move two points together from one cell to another cell.

196
00:17:53.240 --> 00:18:00.290
The source count decreased by two, and the destination count increased by two. Every other count stays unchanged.

197
00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:03.770
The source and the target cells can be neighboring or far apart.

198
00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:09.690
The previous argument shows that this transition changes f by at most the cell size.

199
00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:13.550
It does not depend on how far apart the two cells are.

200
00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:18.720
Such transitions are allowed whenever the source cell contains at least two points.

201
00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:23.350
Now, combine the two types of transition into the random walk.

202
00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:29.210
One point can move to a neighboring cell, and two points can move together to any other cell.

203
00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:37.770
With the partition described here, the paper shows the random walk shown in the image mixes fast enough to give the Poincaré inequality,

204
00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.590
with the constant independent of n, which gives the first inequality.

205
00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:49.690
The small change in f at each allowed transition gives the second inequality, which finishes the proof.

206
00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:53.690
This video is meant to convey intuition and heuristics.

207
00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:58.510
Many objects and steps have only been described informally.

208
00:18:58.820 --> 00:19:04.130
And we have skipped many details, so some connections may still feel abstract.

209
00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:12.090
For example, what are the transition rates of random walk, and why is the multinomial distribution its stationary distribution?

210
00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:18.410
For more detailed intuition, I encourage you to read proof strategy section in the paper.

211
00:19:19.060 --> 00:19:26.710
For the full argument, especially how the combined walk gives the Poincaré inequality, please see the detailed proofs in the paper.
